'''Woria''' is a nearly extinct [[Papuan language]] of the [[Indonesia]]n province of [[Papua (province)|Papua]], on the eastern shore of [[Cenderawasih Bay]]. It is spoken in Botawa village, [[Waropen Regency]], where the Lakes Plain language [[Saponi language|Saponi]] was also spoken.<ref name="Ethnologue22-ID">{{cite web |url=https://www.ethnologue.com/country/ID/languages |title=Indonesia languages |work=[[Ethnologue]]: Languages of the World |edition=22nd |editor1-last=Eberhard |editor1-first=David M. |editor2-last=Simons |editor2-first=Gary F. |editor3-last=Fennig |editor3-first=Charles D. |date=2019 |location=Dallas |publisher=[[SIL International]]}}</ref>

Woria is lexically similar to the [[East Geelvink Bay languages]] and presumably belongs in that family, but is too poorly attested to be sure.

